  "summary": "A Japanese architectural movement that envisioned cities as organic, living systems.",
  "description": "It features megastructures with interchangeable parts, plug-in units, and organic forms that could adapt and grow like living organisms. This emerged from post-war Japanese reconstruction efforts and was influenced by biological metaphors and traditional Japanese concepts of impermanence.",
  "culturalContext": "Metabolism (1960-1973) — Kiyonori Kikutake, Kisho Kurokawa, Fumihiko Maki and Kenzo Tange's Japanese avant-garde — imagined cities as living organisms: capsules swapping into megastructure trunks (Nakagin Capsule Tower, 1972), marine cities floating on rings, Tange's Tokyo Bay Plan. Launched at Tokyo World Design Conference 1960 with the manifesto-like \"Ocean City\", it made Japan the pole of future-city imagination until oil shocks stalled construction; the capsule tower's 2022 demolition intensified global preservation debate over the movement's remaining ideals.",
